Why: two programs open at once must each decode their own bytes. A
SleighArchitecture keeps one translator (Sleigh) per language in a static
map and, for each new Architecture of that language, resets it to the new
Architecture's load image and context database (buildTranslator). An
earlier Architecture still open then decodes its instructions from the
newer one's bytes, and from freed memory once that one is deleted: in wasm,
"table index is out of bounds". Ghidra's console and Java side only ever
use one Architecture at a time. This adds Sleigh::resume, which points the
translator back at an Architecture it was initialized for; reset() alone
leaves it without a disassembly cache, and initialize() would register the
context variables a second time, which the context database refuses once it
holds values. The host layer calls it before decompiling with a program
that is not the translator's last (host/src/program.cc). Building a
translator per Architecture instead costs each program another 120 ms and
18 MB of decoded SLEIGH tables.
Upstream: not reported; upstream uses one Architecture at a time.

